// Stability criteria — dated, pre-registered (METHODOLOGY R6, engine.md).
//
// GENERATION 1, registered 2026-07-31, for a scenario that did not exist before
// today. `version: 1` is this FILE's generation; the thresholds in it are the
// corpus's generation-4 values, copied verbatim from data/scenarios/lr_00 and
// data/scenarios/tracer_2r without one digit changed. That is deliberate and it
// is the whole R6 argument for this file:
//
//   NOT ONE THRESHOLD HERE WAS CHOSEN WITH KNOWLEDGE OF WHAT THIS SCENARIO DOES.
//   The tape and this file were written in the same sitting, before the engine
//   had ever been pointed at solv_1g. Every number was fixed earlier, against
//   the synthetic healthy/sick series in tests/test_09_detectors.rs, to score a
//   corpus this world is not part of. If solv_1g fails one of these, the finding
//   is that solv_1g fails a bar the rest of the corpus is already held to — not
//   that the bar was wrong.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE ASSERTS, AND — MORE IMPORTANTLY — WHAT IT DOES NOT.
// It asserts stability: a region is stable when its metrics stay inside a band
// and their wobble does not grow.
//
//   DEAD            FracBelow(Velocity < 0.003, 80%)   is anything trading?
//   DEAD_EMPLOYMENT FracBelow(Employment < 0.05, 80%)  is anyone working?
//   UNSTABLE        RollingCv(window 10, max 0.12)     short-term chatter
//   DRIFTING        LevelRange(max_ratio: 2.2)         did the level stay put?
//   SWINGING        ResidualDamping(max_ratio: 1.5)    did the wobble grow?
//   CURRENCY_DRAIN  MeanAndSlope(0.82, 0.0)            cash pooling in firms?
//   POP_DESTITUTION FracBelow(RealIncome < 0.8, 50%)   can the pop eat?
//   DEAD_BUILDING   FracBelow(BldUtil < 0.05, 60%)     warning only
//
// IT DOES NOT ASSERT CORRECTNESS, and on this scenario that gap is the point.
// EVERY ONE OF THESE CLASSES PASSES ON A WORLD WHOSE PRICES ARE ARBITRARILY
// WRONG, as long as they are wrong quietly: a run that instantly settles on
// RealWage = 0.02 and holds it scores LevelRange 1.00, RollingCv 0.00 and
// ResidualDamping 0.00 — a clean sweep. What says whether solv_1g's prices went
// to the RIGHT PLACE is equilibrium.ron beside this file, read by
// tests/test_12_solvable.rs, and B8 in the run certificate. This file is the
// stability half of the verdict and nothing more, and it is registered here so
// the two halves can disagree in public.
//
// WHY THE WINDOW IS 150..1000. Copied from lr_00 unchanged. On this scenario
// the transient is arguably zero — mode A starts AT the equilibrium, so there
// is no startup to discard — but shortening the window would be a
// scenario-specific choice made by someone who knew the answer, and it would
// stop these numbers being comparable with the corpus's. 850 scored samples is
// also what the corpus's detectors were characterised on.
//
// WHY EACH THRESHOLD IS WHAT IT IS, in one line each, all inherited:
//   2.2 and 1.5   fixed from the separation between synthetic healthy and sick
//                 series in tests/test_09_detectors.rs, before any scenario was
//                 scored: healthy levels reach at most 1.81 and sick start at
//                 2.69; healthy oscillation reaches 1.04 and sick starts at 1.99.
//   0.003, 0.05,  liveness floors ported from notebooks/07_stability_suite.py,
//   0.12, 0.82,   where they were global constants applied to every scenario.
//   0.8, 0.60
//
// WHERE THE FIRST-PRINCIPLES JUSTIFICATION RUNS OUT, SAID PLAINLY RATHER THAN
// PAPERED OVER. Two of these thresholds are NOT derivable from anything, and
// this scenario is the first world in the repo where that can be demonstrated
// instead of suspected:
//
//   * DEAD's Velocity floor of 0.003. solv_1g's equilibrium Velocity is
//     20/105 = 0.190, but §2.2 of docs/design/solvable-scenarios.md proves the
//     price LEVEL is unpinned in this tape (one wealth tier -> the pop's cash
//     rule is skipped -> the excess-demand system is homogeneous of degree
//     zero), and Velocity is proportional to the level at fixed money stock. So
//     the equilibrium Velocity is any number in (0, 0.190]: a run with PERFECT
//     relative prices can score DEAD, and a run can escape DEAD by inflating.
//     A threshold on Velocity is therefore scoring an unpinned direction of the
//     dynamics here. It is left at the corpus value anyway, because changing it
//     for this scenario would be exactly the fitted-threshold move R6 forbids —
//     and because the pair (solv_1g, solv_1g_money) exists precisely to measure
//     how much difference the anchor makes. Read a DEAD or DRIFTING hit on
//     Velocity in this scenario against that caveat.
//   * DRIFTING's 2.2x on Velocity, for the same reason.
//   Both caveats are about the METRIC, not the number; no number would fix them.
//   The RealWage arm of DRIFTING has no such problem: RealWage is a ratio of two
//   prices and is invariant to the level.
//
// CHANGING A THRESHOLD AFTER SEEING RESULTS REQUIRES A NEW DATED GENERATION,
// never a silent edit. `criteria_sha` in the run manifest records which bytes
// actually scored a given run.
(
    version: 1,
    date: "2026-07-31",
    transient: 150,
    analysis_end: 1000,
    staple_good: "grain",
    labour_good: "labour",
    nan_policy: FailClosed,
    classes: [
        // A region with no transaction velocity is not an economy. Checked first
        // and short-circuits: once dead, nothing else about it is meaningful.
        (
            name: "DEAD",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.003, frac: 0.80),
            window: Analysis,
            short_circuit: true,
        ),
        (
            name: "DEAD_EMPLOYMENT",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Employment],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.05, frac: 0.80),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Short-term oscillation: the rolling coefficient of variation.
        (
            name: "UNSTABLE",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity, Employment, RealWage],
            rule: RollingCv(window: 10, max_cv: 0.12),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Did the level stay put? The 95th/5th-percentile band across the
        // window, so a metric that went somewhere and came back is as unstable
        // as one that went and stayed, and one freak sample cannot decide it.
        (
            name: "DRIFTING",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity, RealWage],
            rule: LevelRange(max_ratio: 2.2),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Did the wobble grow? Measured on the detrended residual with an
        // interquartile spread, so this asks only about oscillation and cannot
        // be flipped by a single tick. A perfectly constant series scores 0.0
        // and passes, which is correct: a constant is not swinging. Whether the
        // constant is the RIGHT constant is equilibrium.ron's question.
        (
            name: "SWINGING",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity, RealWage],
            rule: ResidualDamping(max_ratio: 1.5),
            window: Halves,
        ),
        // Currency pooling in buildings and still trending that way. At this
        // scenario's equilibrium Concentration is 75/105 = 0.714 with slope 0,
        // so the class rests inside its bar on BOTH conditions, not just one.
        (
            name: "CURRENCY_DRAIN",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Concentration],
            rule: MeanAndSlope(mean_min: 0.82, slope_min: 0.0),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        (
            name: "POP_DESTITUTION",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [RealIncome],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.8, frac: 0.50),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Warning only: an idle plant is a symptom, not a failed region.
        (
            name: "DEAD_BUILDING",
            fatal: false,
            metrics: [BldUtil],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.05, frac: 0.60),
            window: PostTransient,
        ),
    ],
)
